window.__finlexDocLoaded(10099,{"id":10099,"articles":[{"id":1000400,"no":"Overview","heading":"Official Overview & Scope (IFRS Foundation)","text":"Borrowing costs that are directly attributable to the acquisition, construction or production of a qualifying asset form part of the cost of that asset. Other borrowing costs are recognised as an expense. Borrowing costs are interest and other costs that an entity incurs in connection with the borrowing of funds. IAS 23 provides guidance on how to measure borrowing costs, particularly when the costs of acquisition, construction or production are funded by an entity’s general borrowings.","seq":0},{"id":1000401,"no":"IAS 23.8–25","heading":"Capitalisation of Borrowing Costs on Qualifying Assets","text":"• Core rule (IAS 23.8): capitalise borrowing costs directly attributable to the acquisition, construction, or production of a qualifying asset (takes a substantial period of time to get ready for intended use/sale).\n• Specific vs. general borrowings (IAS 23.12–14): specific borrowings = actual borrowing costs minus temporary investment income; general borrowings = weighted-average capitalisation rate applied to asset expenditures.\n• Commencement, suspension & cessation (IAS 23.17–25): start when expenditures, borrowing costs, and preparation activities are all underway; suspend during extended interruptions; cease when substantially all activities are complete (converged with China CAS 17).","seq":1},{"id":1000402,"no":"History","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In April 2001 the International Accounting Standards Board (Board) adopted IAS 23 Borrowing Costs, which had originally been issued by the International Accounting Standards Committee in December 1993. IAS 23 Borrowing Costs replaced IAS 23 Capitalisation of Borrowing Costs (issued in March 1984).\n\nIn March 2007 the Board issued a revised IAS 23 that eliminated the option of immediate recognition of borrowing costs as an expense.\n\nOther Standards have made minor consequential amendments to IAS 23. They include Agriculture: Bearer Plants (Amendments to IAS 16 and IAS 41) (issued June 2014), IFRS 9 Financial Instruments (issued July 2014), IFRS 16 Leases (issued January 2016) and Annual Improvements to IFRS Standards 2015–2017 Cycle (issued December 2017).","seq":2},{"id":1000403,"no":"Support","heading":"Related Projects & Implementation Support","text":"[Related completed projects]\n• Borrowing Costs (2007 revision of IAS 23)\n• Borrowing costs eligible for capitalisation (Amendments to IAS 23)\n• Borrowing costs on land (IAS 23)\n• Expenditures on a qualifying asset (IAS 23)\n• Over Time Transfer of Constructed Good (IAS 23)\n\n[Implementation support]\n• IAS 23 supporting material","seq":3},{"id":1000404,"no":"References","heading":"Official Text Link & Public Practical Guidance","text":"Official IFRS Foundation Page (English): https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ias-23-borrowing-costs/\nAccess Note: Free access to the unaccompanied text of IFRS Accounting Standards on ifrs.org requires a free registered IFRS.org account; Basis for Conclusions and Illustrative Examples require an IFRS Digital Subscription.\nPublic Companion Overview: KPMG Insights into IFRS — An Overview (Sept 2026): https://assets.kpmg.com/content/dam/kpmgsites/xx/pdf/ifrg/2026/isg-2026-insights-an-overview.pdf","seq":4}]});
